From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: a stretch with nothing to draw does not divide by zero

MeediOS ended -- on a click on David's Latitude, fifteen seconds after its
start on its developer's machine -- with exit code 0x94: an integer divide
by zero at win32u.so+0x204f9 (the same place in every build since 10.0-148),
on win32u's Unix side, where the signal cannot become an exception the
program could catch ("Exception frame is not in stack limits").

That is intersect_vis_rectangles' scaling, dividing by the source's width.
StretchBlt (and AlphaBlend) from a DC without a device rectangle -- the
screen's -- lets an empty source through the clipping (clip_device_rect
copies it), and a source of no width with a destination of some width is
taken for a stretch. A source or destination of no width or height now has
nothing to draw, as on Windows.

Test: test/stretchzero-gate.sh with test/stretchzero-probe.c (mutant
SG_MUTANT_STRETCH_DIVIDES_BY_ZERO; 10.0-148 ends with 0x94 there).
---
--- a/dlls/win32u/bitblt.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/bitblt.c
@@ -55,6 +55,14 @@
     }
     else  /* stretching */
     {
+        /* Stained Glass OS: a source or a destination of no width or height
+         * has nothing to draw. The scaling below divided by it -- a source
+         * DC without a device rectangle (the screen) lets an empty source
+         * through -- and the signal in win32u's Unix side ended the program
+         * (MeediOS, 2026-10-03: exit code 0x94). */
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_STRETCH_DIVIDES_BY_ZERO
+        if (!src->width || !src->height || !dst->width || !dst->height) return FALSE;
+#endif
         /* map source rectangle into destination coordinates */
         rect = src->visrect;
         OffsetRect( &rect,
